1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,718
Treat y as a function of x.

2
00:00:01,988 --> 00:00:08,327
The middle term is a product of x squared and y squared, so it needs a product rule as well as a chain rule.

3
00:00:09,447 --> 00:00:12,803
The derivative of y to the fifth includes y prime.

4
00:00:13,073 --> 00:00:18,425
In the middle term, differentiate x squared for one term and y squared for the other.

5
00:00:18,695 --> 00:00:21,423
The constant twelve differentiates to zero.

6
00:00:22,543 --> 00:00:25,724
Collect the terms containing y prime on one side.

7
00:00:25,994 --> 00:00:30,336
Factor out y prime, then move the remaining terms to the other side.

8
00:00:31,456 --> 00:00:35,682
Divide by the coefficient of y prime, wherever it is nonzero.

9
00:00:35,952 --> 00:00:41,421
This gives the slope along the implicit curve at points where that expression defines a derivative.

10
00:00:42,541 --> 00:00:44,840
Here y is an unknown function of x.

11
00:00:45,110 --> 00:00:51,804
Differentiate both sides with respect to x, and attach y prime whenever the chain rule differentiates a y

12
00:00:51,804 --> 00:00:52,505
expression.

13
00:00:53,625 --> 00:00:57,921
The derivative of y to the fourth is four y cubed times y prime.

14
00:00:58,191 --> 00:00:59,514
Solve for y prime.

15
00:00:59,784 --> 00:01:06,392
This formula requires y to be nonzero; it describes the slope where the curve is locally a differentiable

16
00:01:06,392 --> 00:01:07,273
function of x.

17
00:01:08,393 --> 00:01:14,186
For the second derivative, write y prime as negative x cubed times y to the negative third power.

18
00:01:14,456 --> 00:01:18,740
Apply the product rule, and again use the chain rule on the y factor.

19
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:23,726
Substitute negative x cubed over y cubed for y prime.

20
00:01:23,996 --> 00:01:25,970
The second term becomes negative.

21
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:30,594
A second derivative should not still contain an unexplained y prime here.

22
00:01:31,714 --> 00:01:33,026
Combine the fractions.

23
00:01:33,296 --> 00:01:39,670
The original equation says x to the fourth plus y to the fourth is sixteen, so the numerator simplifies.

24
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:44,166
Reusing the original relationship often makes implicit answers much cleaner.

25
00:01:45,286 --> 00:01:47,642
We could use an implicit quotient rule.

26
00:01:47,912 --> 00:01:51,906
A simpler option is to multiply by the nonzero denominator first.

27
00:01:52,176 --> 00:01:55,961
Keep its restriction because multiplying can hide an excluded point.

28
00:01:57,081 --> 00:02:01,597
Expand the right side, move its y term to the left, and factor y.

29
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:03,992
Now we can solve explicitly.

30
00:02:05,112 --> 00:02:09,361
The new denominator x squared plus two never vanishes for real x.

31
00:02:09,631 --> 00:02:16,796
But the original denominator equals x over x squared plus two, so the original equation excludes x equal to

32
00:02:16,796 --> 00:02:17,131
zero.

33
00:02:18,251 --> 00:02:21,421
Differentiate the explicit quotient and simplify.

34
00:02:21,691 --> 00:02:27,902
The original curve still excludes zero, even though the simplified derivative expression has a value there.

35
00:02:29,022 --> 00:02:30,717
Both terms are products.

36
00:02:30,987 --> 00:02:35,167
Cosine y also needs the chain rule because y depends on x.

37
00:02:36,287 --> 00:02:39,689
Differentiate x cosine y with the product rule.

38
00:02:39,959 --> 00:02:43,453
Differentiate y cosine x with another product rule.

39
00:02:43,723 --> 00:02:47,241
Notice which derivatives create y prime and which do not.

40
00:02:48,361 --> 00:02:50,126
Collect y prime terms.

41
00:02:50,396 --> 00:02:54,262
Move the other terms to the opposite side, keeping both signs.

42
00:02:55,382 --> 00:02:58,842
Divide by the remaining coefficient where it is nonzero.

43
00:02:59,112 --> 00:03:05,694
Keeping the implicit variables in the answer is expected; there is no need to solve the original equation for y.

44
00:03:06,814 --> 00:03:12,956
Both the base and exponent depend on x, so the ordinary constant exponent power rule does not apply.

45
00:03:13,226 --> 00:03:19,287
We work where the base is positive, giving a real logarithm and the usual smooth variable power.

46
00:03:20,407 --> 00:03:23,146
Take natural logarithms of both sides.

47
00:03:23,416 --> 00:03:25,553
The exponent comes down in front.

48
00:03:25,823 --> 00:03:30,594
This turns a difficult variable power into a product that we know how to differentiate.

49
00:03:31,714 --> 00:03:34,489
The left side becomes y prime over y.

50
00:03:34,759 --> 00:03:37,000
On the right, use the product rule.

51
00:03:37,270 --> 00:03:42,761
The logarithm also needs the chain rule, producing six x squared over the inside.

52
00:03:43,881 --> 00:03:47,643
Multiply by y, then replace y with the original function.

53
00:03:47,913 --> 00:03:51,048
Forgetting this final multiplication is a common mistake.

54
00:03:51,318 --> 00:03:56,612
Logarithmic differentiation is especially useful when a variable appears in the exponent.

55
00:03:57,732 --> 00:04:02,585
Both base and exponent vary, so use logarithmic differentiation again.

56
00:04:02,855 --> 00:04:06,918
Work where tangent x is positive so the real logarithm is defined.

57
00:04:08,038 --> 00:04:11,278
Take a logarithm and bring the sine x exponent in front.

58
00:04:11,548 --> 00:04:13,579
The right side is now a product.

59
00:04:14,699 --> 00:04:16,859
Differentiate with the product rule.

60
00:04:17,129 --> 00:04:21,424
The logarithm of tangent has derivative secant squared divided by tangent.

61
00:04:22,544 --> 00:04:24,518
Multiply by the original y.

62
00:04:24,788 --> 00:04:32,093
The second term inside the brackets simplifies to secant x because sine x times secant squared x divided by

63
00:04:32,093 --> 00:04:34,482
tangent x equals one over cosine x.

64
00:04:35,602 --> 00:04:38,366
A tangent line needs the point and its slope.

65
00:04:38,636 --> 00:04:43,164
Check the point: six divided by three is two, so it lies on the graph.

66
00:04:44,284 --> 00:04:48,730
Differentiate with the quotient rule, then evaluate at x equal to six.

67
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,183
The derivative gives the tangent slope.

68
00:04:52,303 --> 00:04:59,071
Use point-slope form, y minus the given y coordinate equals the slope times x minus the given x coordinate.

69
00:04:59,341 --> 00:05:02,604
This form is already a complete tangent-line equation.

70
00:05:03,724 --> 00:05:08,890
At pi over six, sine is one half, so twice sine equals the given height one.

71
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:12,239
The derivative of sine is cosine.

72
00:05:12,509 --> 00:05:18,814
Cosine pi over six is square root three over two, so multiplying by two gives square root three.

73
00:05:19,934 --> 00:05:23,092
Substitute the point and slope into point-slope form.

74
00:05:23,362 --> 00:05:26,125
Keep pi over six exact instead of rounding it.

75
00:05:27,245 --> 00:05:29,660
The angle in the cosine term is two x.

76
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:33,912
That inside factor will contribute a two when we differentiate.

77
00:05:35,032 --> 00:05:37,029
Differentiate sine normally.

78
00:05:37,299 --> 00:05:42,465
Cosine two x becomes negative sine two x, times the inside derivative two.

79
00:05:43,585 --> 00:05:48,078
At x equal to pi over six, the doubled angle is pi over three.

80
00:05:48,348 --> 00:05:52,063
Both trigonometric values here are square root three over two.

81
00:05:53,183 --> 00:05:55,796
Use the negative slope with the given point.

82
00:05:56,066 --> 00:05:58,864
Do not forget the factor two from the doubled angle.

83
00:05:59,984 --> 00:06:03,037
Rewrite the quotient as a product with a negative exponent.

84
00:06:03,307 --> 00:06:07,127
This lets the product and chain rules handle the derivative cleanly.

85
00:06:08,247 --> 00:06:15,387
The derivative of the second factor is negative five times the inside to the negative six, times negative two x.

86
00:06:15,657 --> 00:06:17,677
The two negative signs cancel.

87
00:06:18,797 --> 00:06:20,481
Use a common denominator.

88
00:06:20,751 --> 00:06:25,754
Three minus x squared plus ten x squared becomes three plus nine x squared.

89
00:06:26,024 --> 00:06:30,610
At x equal to two, the denominator is one and the numerator is thirty nine.

90
00:06:31,730 --> 00:06:34,842
The tangent line is steep and increasing at this point.

91
00:06:35,112 --> 00:06:39,222
Use y plus two because the given y coordinate is negative two.

92
00:06:40,342 --> 00:06:44,870
This curve is implicit, so find the slope with implicit differentiation.

93
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:50,620
The point satisfies the equation: twenty five sixteenths minus nine sixteenths equals one.

94
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:53,864
Differentiate both squares.

95
00:06:54,134 --> 00:06:56,294
The y square needs y prime.

96
00:06:56,564 --> 00:06:59,513
Solve the resulting linear equation for y prime.

97
00:07:00,633 --> 00:07:03,709
Evaluate both x and y at the given point.

98
00:07:03,979 --> 00:07:07,590
An implicit derivative usually depends on both coordinates.

99
00:07:08,710 --> 00:07:11,996
Substitute the point and slope into point-slope form.

100
00:07:12,266 --> 00:07:16,689
The x expression is x plus five because the x coordinate is negative five.

101
00:07:17,809 --> 00:07:21,872
We need a product rule on the right and an implicit chain rule on the left.

102
00:07:22,992 --> 00:07:27,474
The first right-hand term differentiates x cubed and keeps two minus x.

103
00:07:27,744 --> 00:07:31,204
The second keeps x cubed and multiplies by negative one.

104
00:07:32,324 --> 00:07:34,622
At one comma one, the slope is one.

105
00:07:34,892 --> 00:07:40,883
Point-slope form gives y minus one equals x minus one, so the tangent is y equals x.
